    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-1526",
      "problem": "Density $D$ is the rate that mass $M$ changes as volume $V$ changes. What are the units of density in terms of base units?",
      "worked_solution": "We *measure the change* in the mass, as *we change* the volume. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{unit of mass}}{\\textrm{unit of volume}}.\n$$\n\nThe base unit for mass is $\\textrm{kg},$ and the base unit for volume is $\\textrm{m}^3.$ Therefore, the units of density are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{m}^3},\n$$\n\nor, kilograms per cubic meter.",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182163/observations/step-e1526-09867deb5a4587d04c52.html",
